{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And if thus You do to me.</b> The word את is a pronoun alluding to the attribute of judgment, and likewise Scripture says ״ויחר אף ה׳ מאד״ — and His wrath refers to that attribute. It is like (Deuteronomy 5:24) ״ואת תדבר אלינו״, and likewise (Ezekiel 28:14) ״את כרוב ממשח הסוכך״, which is a pronoun referring to the second cherub, which is called כבוד, for the two cherubim are כבוד and תפארת, as I explained in the section ויקחו לי תרומה. For in a Torah scroll that is not vowelized, a person could read ואם ככה אתּ עשה לי, with a qamatz under the shin, for when the letters are not vowelized they bear many meanings and divide into many sparks, and because of this we were commanded not to vowelize a Torah scroll, for the meaning of each and every word depends on the vowelization, and with vowels its meaning is only one matter, whereas unvowelized a person can understand in it many wondrous, great, and weighty matters — and understand this, for you will need it in many places. For example (Genesis 22:12) ״עתה ידעתי כי ירא אלהים אתה״, where a person could read עתה יִדַעתי with a ḥiriq under the yod and a dagesh in the dalet, from the language of (Job 38:12) ״ידעת השחר מקומו״, its meaning being: now I have made known in the world, through this commandment that I commanded you and you fulfilled, that you are a fearer of Elohim. And likewise (Exodus 2:5) ״ותשלח את אמתה ותקחה״, which is the language of a maidservant, whereas we could read אֱמֶתָהּ, from the language of (Deuteronomy 3:11) ״באמת איש״ — and so the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, understood it in the Midrash, where they said: אשתרבובי אשתרבב, 'stretch out your arm and be stretched out.' And many others besides these." "<b>Kill me, I pray, a killing.</b> It may be said that the doubling alludes to the first occurrence, that is: let my killing now be at Your hand, not at the hand of flesh and blood, for he feared lest Israel kill him." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״הרגני נא הרוג״ — Moses said before the Holy One, blessed be He: Master of the universe, kill the one to be killed! If I speak to Israel, they will kill me; and if I do not carry out Your mission, I am liable to the death penalty. Therefore either way I am to be killed — kill the one to be killed!"], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The Hebrew includes a Latin 'S' intruding into the word יִSַעתי, evidently a typographical or encoding artifact in the source; rendered as though it were simply יִדַעתי."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:17.831969665Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}